WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:04.080
A lack of curiosity just might kill this cat.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.090
Two of the producers of the upcoming film
adaptation of the venerable Broadway play

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:12.940
Cats have finally surfaced to comment on the
massive backlash to the trailer's nightmarish

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.269
visual aesthetic.

00:00:14.269 --> 00:00:18.110
Eric Fellner and Tim Bevan are the heads of
Universal Pictures' partnering production

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:23.020
company Working Title, and in a recent conversation
with Variety, the pair let the cat out of

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:27.260
the bag regarding reactions to the spot, and
their hope that the flick will find its audience

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.050
among true fans of the play.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:30.050
Fellner said:

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:31.320
"What does one say?

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.239
The reality is that 100 million people or
more saw the trailer, and, yes, there were

00:00:35.239 --> 00:00:39.229
some people that didn't like it, and as is
the world we live in, those who didn't like

00:00:39.229 --> 00:00:41.100
it were the most vociferous."

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.519
Bevan then jumped in to opine that those who
love stage productions of Cats, which typically

00:00:45.519 --> 00:00:49.960
feature actors wearing makeup that does not
make them look like terrifying monstrosities,

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.769
totally got what the filmmakers were going
for - and also that those who were turned

00:00:53.769 --> 00:00:58.609
off by the movie's spot must have been expecting
to see real, actual cats singing and dancing,

00:00:58.609 --> 00:01:00.209
or maybe a cartoon.

00:01:00.209 --> 00:01:01.209
He asserted:

00:01:01.209 --> 00:01:05.239
"[The viewers who criticized the trailer]
were probably people who didn't know Cats,

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:09.159
and the fact is they were either anticipating
something animated or something that was on

00:01:09.159 --> 00:01:10.540
four legs.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.329
Among people who know Cats, the reaction was
pretty solid."

00:01:13.329 --> 00:01:14.380
Yikes!

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:16.729
Those are a couple of dubious assumptions,
fella.

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:19.310
"Do you know how foolish you sound right now?"

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:24.450
It is true that there were comments from just
such users scattered about the Twitterverse,

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:27.719
comments along the lines of, "It's Cats...
what did you expect?"

00:01:27.719 --> 00:01:32.499
But they were severely outnumbered by remarks
expressing abject horror at the spot, and

00:01:32.499 --> 00:01:35.829
wondering how on Earth the filmmakers could
have failed to notice that they were fielding

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:40.930
a picture that didn't so much invoke the whimsy
and emotion of the play as it did Cronenbergian

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:41.930
body horror.

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:45.780
Now, it's worth noting that the producers
claim that the visuals presented in the trailer

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:50.049
were not exactly finished, and that numerous
small tweaks have been applied since the spot

00:01:50.049 --> 00:01:51.569
was released back in July.

00:01:51.569 --> 00:01:56.350
We submit, however, that it's going to take
a heck of a lot more than "subtle changes"

00:01:56.350 --> 00:02:01.280
to haul viewers up out of the Uncanny Valley,
and get their butts in the seats for Cats.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:06.889
You could say that such a prospect would take
a little bit of… "meow-gic."

00:02:06.889 --> 00:02:08.310
"MEEEEEEoooooow."

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:11.810
The casual observer could be forgiven for
thinking that we're ringing a few too many

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:12.890
alarm bells here.

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:17.819
After all, Cats has a pretty sterling pedigree;
according to Playbill, it's the tenth-highest-grossing

00:02:17.819 --> 00:02:22.099
Broadway musical of all time, which is certainly
nothing to sneeze at.

00:02:22.099 --> 00:02:26.200
The movie adaptation, it must also be said,
boasts a staggering amount of talent.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:31.170
It stars: Taylor Swift, one of the most bankable
celebrities of any stripe on the planet; Idris

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:36.330
Elba, one of the most charismatic and beloved
actors alive; Sir Ian McKellen, the definition

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:41.220
of a veteran actor and a consummate pro; and
Dame Judi Dench, whose title alone should

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:44.810
tell you all you need to know about just how
well-regarded she is.

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:49.840
The flick was co-written by Lee Hall, who
penned the excellent Elton John biopic Rocketman,

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:54.500
and directed by Tom Hooper, who fielded the
well-regarded and financially successful 2012

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:59.030
Broadway adaptation Les Misérables, and who
won the Academy Award for Best Director for

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:00.430
The King's Speech.

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:05.409
On paper, all of the ingredients for success
are there - but movies aren't made on paper,

00:03:05.409 --> 00:03:10.340
and Cats represents a perfect storm of baffling
miscalculations which seem destined to ensure

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:14.270
that its inevitable failure won't be noteworthy;
it will be legendary.

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:19.580
First, consider that Cats' story, while appropriate
for a flashy Broadway production, doesn't

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:21.329
translate very well to film.

00:03:21.329 --> 00:03:25.799
Don't take our word for it: it's been attempted
once already, with a 1998 direct-to-video

00:03:25.799 --> 00:03:30.140
feature which was a highly faithful adaptation
of the stage production, and which currently

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:33.319
sits at 20% on review aggregator Rotten Tomatoes.

00:03:33.319 --> 00:03:38.140
But, c'mon - we can all agree that even the
Broadway version is… pretty out there.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:41.870
"We just do some poppers and say whatever
comes to mind.

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:45.209
Jellicle, Griddlebone, Mungojerrie, Jimmy
McCracklins!"

00:03:45.209 --> 00:03:50.020
Second, while the Broadway production certainly
has its fans, it's not at all clear that their

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:55.459
numbers are substantial enough to spell success
for a $100 million picture which - considering

00:03:55.459 --> 00:04:00.099
its holiday release frame - is sure to have
a pretty massive advertising budget.

00:04:00.099 --> 00:04:03.269
Third: oh, those horrifying visuals.

00:04:03.269 --> 00:04:08.390
Even if Swift, Elba, and company turn in performances
for the ages, your picture - to paraphrase

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:13.099
the late, great Roger Ebert - is in deep trouble
if the audience doesn't even enjoy the primary

00:04:13.099 --> 00:04:14.989
act of looking at it.

00:04:14.989 --> 00:04:20.010
Fourth, and perhaps even more importantly:
the movie is being released on December 20,

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:24.190
the same date as a tiny little picture which
you may have heard of by the name of Star

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:26.320
Wars: The Rise of Skywalker.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.260
Even being released in the same month as the
final film of the Skywalker Saga would have

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:34.530
spelled trouble for a movie like Cats; that
Universal chose to release it on the same

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:36.850
day is nothing short of lunacy.

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:40.670
We feel safe in predicting right now that
Cats won't just be one of the biggest flops

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:45.540
of 2019; it will be one of the biggest flops
of this swiftly waning decade, and there's

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:50.100
nothing that the flick's producers, stars,
or anyone else can do to mitigate the disaster

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.100
to come.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:54.670
Talent-wise, this picture might have the pick
of the litter… but it's nine lives might

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:56.300
be up before they even start.

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:58.510
OK, yeah… we'll see ourselves out…

